{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788975783,"nonce":"6WgCs/9TTgrEzczWGz5evq6FPrE=","title":"A failed compiled invoke re-runs the function, doubling side effects","message":"`call_function` dispatches to compiled code and, when the invoke returns `Err`,\nrecords a fallback and calls `execute_hl_function` anyway. That is only sound if\na failed invoke did nothing, and it is not: compiled code can run to completion\nand fail in return marshaling, or fail partway through. Anything it already did\nhappens twice.\n\nObserved as output duplicated in place, e.g. `alloc: 100alloc: 100`, with\n`[tiered] fallback findex=29 reason=compiled invoke failed`.\n\nFix: distinguish a failure BEFORE entering the function (bad signature, no entry\npoint), which is safe to retry, from one INSIDE it, which must propagate. Until\nthey are distinguishable, any tier that declines mid-call can corrupt output.","files":null}]}